--- body ---
## Context

The author's proposal of 2026-08-30 (`spec/DECISIONS.md`, awaiting
ratification) makes every vector file certify its own embedding chain through
a calibration header, on the invariant that no file mixes chains. That
invariant implies a format, and this ticket is that format.

The data model already exists and is not in question: `spec/CONSTRAINTS.md`
C1's three links, and `spec/DESIGN.md` §2.1's four stage keys. What is missing
is that the model lives as a schema INSIDE zoteus's index rather than as a
portable artifact, and the cost of that coupling is measured rather than
speculative. Ticket 0025's substrate correction records every index on the
author's machine dying on open against the renamed schema
(`virtual tables may not be indexed`), forcing a full rebuild before X2, X3a
and X4's real-corpus arms could run at all; ticket 0100 is still open for two
drivers pinned to the same pre-rename schema. A corpus that outlives the index
schema is what stops that recurring.

Three further consumers want it. Adopt-a-foreign-index (the awaiting entry in
`spec/DECISIONS.md`) needs a file a stranger can verify. `GOVERNANCE.md`
commits the fork to archival once the train resolves, so the corpus must be
readable without it. And the second machine cannot reuse the first machine's
work today: R30's designated GPU host and the author's CPU host each rebuild
what the other already has.

## Shape

Three layers, separable, each independently reusable, each keyed by the layer
below it.

1. **text** — content-addressed by `text_hash`, already defined in
   `spec/DESIGN.md` §2.1.
2. **chunks** — byte offsets into that text, never copies of it. Zotero core
   reaches the same answer (chunk location as start/end offsets, content
   re-derived from the source pack); it is described in `verification/FIELD-REVIEW.md`
   and reimplemented from the description, never copied — the repository is
   AGPL-3.0 and zoteus is MIT.
3. **vectors** — one contiguous slab with stored norms, the geometry X1
   already settled by measurement (`bench/results/0025-x1-timing/`: per-row
   BLOBs miss the budget by roughly an order of magnitude where the slab meets
   it). Binary codes ride alongside as a second artifact
   (`bench/results/0025-x1-recall/`), not as a replacement.

The manifest carries the calibration header, the resolved chain (model id,
dtype, pooling, normalize, input template, dims — every axis
`bench/models.json` declares, per ticket 0486's class), the segmenter and
chunker keys, and the schema version. Calibration vectors live in the manifest
section and NOT in the slab's row space, so no consumer can return one as a
hit.

Format: a raw little-endian float32 slab plus a JSON manifest, readable from
both languages `bench/` already uses with no new dependency. Arrow, Parquet
and HDF5 all buy less than they cost here and each adds a reader the acceptance
harness would have to carry.

## Sequencing

Blocked by 0485, and the dependency is real rather than procedural: 0485
prices cosine against task recall, which decides what the calibration header
must be compared ON, and therefore what the manifest has to store. Designing
the layers ahead of that verdict is fine; freezing the header's contract is
not. The awaiting entry in `spec/DECISIONS.md` must also be ratified before
the never-mix invariant is built to — it changes `spec/DESIGN.md` §2.1 and
§2.2.

## Actions

1. Specify the manifest, the slab layout, and the three keys, in
   `spec/DESIGN.md` §2.2 rather than here — this ticket carries the work, the
   owning document carries the schema.
2. Write a reader and a writer in each of the two languages, sharing no code,
   so the format is proven portable rather than asserted portable.
3. Convert one real corpus to it and re-run a driver against the converted
   file, retiring that driver's dependence on the index schema (0100's
   destination).
4. Record the applied registry axes in the manifest from
   `bench/registry.mjs` / `bench/registry.py`, never from a literal — ticket
   0486's class guard applies here the moment the manifest exists.

## Test

- Round trip: write, read back in the OTHER language, and reproduce the slab
  byte for byte and the manifest field for field.
- Negative: a file whose manifest declares one chain and whose calibration
  vectors were produced by another must be REJECTED by the reader, not merely
  flagged.
- The converted corpus must reproduce a committed driver result within that
  driver's stated noise, or the conversion lost something.

## Verification

- [ ] Reader/writer pairs in both languages, neither sharing code with the other
- [ ] A real corpus converted and a driver re-run against it
- [ ] The chain-mismatch rejection demonstrated red before it is green

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The format specified in `spec/DESIGN.md` §2.2, this ticket holding only
      the work
- [ ] One driver reads the portable file instead of the index schema
- [ ] The manifest's chain fields come from the registry, with no literal
      anywhere
